---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { indexablePage } from "../lib/metadata";

const metadata = indexablePage({
  title: "About Atrinik: Human imagination and open development",
  description:
    "Atrinik began in 2009 as a human-developed open-source world. Learn how human creative direction, agentic software work, and direct contributions shape it today.",
  canonicalPath: "/about/",
  socialImageId: "atrinik-then",
});
---

<Base metadata={metadata}>
  <header class="page-hero">
    <p class="eyebrow">About Atrinik</p>
    <h1 class="page-title">Human imagination. Agentic engineering.</h1>
    <p class="page-lede">
      Atrinik keeps people in creative control of its game world and reserves
      final visual and audio assets for human creators. Codex-driven workflows
      primarily build and maintain the software that brings that craft to life,
      and direct human code contributions are welcome.
    </p>
  </header>

  <section class="section" aria-labelledby="history-heading">
    <div class="section-heading">
      <p class="eyebrow">From 2009 to today</p>
      <h2 id="history-heading">A human-developed beginning</h2>
      <p>
        Atrinik began in 2009 under founder and original lead Zoey Rose. The
        2011 releases were a major era of development, not the project's origin.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="detail-grid">
      <article class="detail-card">
        <h3>Founded in 2009</h3>
        <p>
          Zoey Rose founded and originally led Atrinik. She worked alongside the
          wider Atrinik Development Team as maps, art, music, quests, dialogue,
          lore, and code grew through the work of many people over the years.
        </p>
        <a
          class="link-arrow"
          href="https://github.com/atrinik/classic/commit/6e5ee3ceb53c8ce3354acd6de08a308fdd21b8da"
        >
          See the initial 2009 source <span aria-hidden="true">→</span>
        </a>
      </article>
      <article class="detail-card">
        <h3>Credit across lineages</h3>
        <p>
          Atrinik's founding sources credited Daimonin and Crossfire. Classic
          also retains work from Atrinik contributors and appropriately licensed
          upstream authors. Their repository notices and per-file records—not a
          blanket claim—remain authoritative.
        </p>
        <a class="link-arrow" href="https://github.com/atrinik/classic">
          Read the Classic history and notices <span aria-hidden="true">→</span>
        </a>
      </article>
      <article class="detail-card">
        <h3>Human creative direction</h3>
        <p>
          Atrinik does not accept generated visual or audio assets as final game
          content. Clearly identified generated art or sound may serve as a
          temporary design drop-in until a suitable human-created replacement is
          found or made. Human content creators may use generative tools to help
          shape maps or develop quests, dialogue, and lore while directing and
          reviewing the result. Owner repositories are documenting the policy
          and auditing the existing corpus before making a blanket authorship
          claim. Human-created upstream work still keeps its own author, source,
          license, and attribution; it is not automatically Atrinik-original.
        </p>
        <a class="link-arrow" href="/licenses/">
          Follow the exact ownership boundaries <span aria-hidden="true">→</span
          >
        </a>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section" aria-labelledby="two-paths-heading">
    <div class="section-heading">
      <p class="eyebrow">Two paths through the same world</p>
      <h2 id="two-paths-heading">Playable history and an MIT future</h2>
      <p>
        The two lines share a project and a setting, but they are separate
        implementations with different licenses and release readiness.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="split-grid">
      <article class="path-card path-card--classic">
        <div class="path-card-topline">
          <span class="path-number">Path I</span>
          <span class="status-tag status-tag--live"
            >Playable and maintained</span
          >
        </div>
        <h3>Classic</h3>
        <p>
          The current playable line is the maintained GPL C lineage of the
          human-developed project, actively coordinated in the unified{" "}<code
            >atrinik/classic</code
          >{" "}repository. Its client, server, editor, libatrinik, and protocol
          share one version, release notes stream, and coordinated artifact set
          under GPL-2.0-or-later. Current code maintenance primarily uses
          Codex-driven workflows.
        </p>
        <ul class="path-details">
          <li>Current playable implementation</li>
          <li>One repository and supported 5.x release line</li>
          <li>GPL-2.0-or-later software</li>
        </ul>
        <div class="card-actions">
          <a class="link-arrow" href="https://github.com/atrinik/classic">
            Explore Atrinik Classic on GitHub <span aria-hidden="true">→</span>
          </a>
        </div>
      </article>

      <article class="path-card path-card--future">
        <div class="path-card-topline">
          <span class="path-number">Path II</span>
          <span class="status-tag">In development</span>
        </div>
        <h3>Next generation</h3>
        <p>
          The replacement is a Codex-driven MIT reimplementation and
          improvement: a Go server, Rust client, editor, renderer and content
          toolkit, plus a new Protobuf protocol. Its repositories use
          evidence-gated provenance: independent implementation is the default,
          while an exact, separable historical Classic contribution may be
          reused at an MIT destination only when a complete-history audit proves
          it fits one applicable grant's past-contribution and sole-authorship
          scope. The Classic repository remains GPL-distributed. These
          foundations do not yet form a complete game release or replace the
          playable Classic line.
        </p>
        <ul class="path-details">
          <li>MIT Go and Rust implementation</li>
          <li>MIT-licensed software</li>
          <li>Not yet available as a complete game</li>
        </ul>
        <div class="card-actions">
          <a
            class="link-arrow"
            href="https://github.com/atrinik/atrinik/issues/168"
          >
            Follow the replacement roadmap <span aria-hidden="true">→</span>
          </a>
        </div>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section" aria-labelledby="open-development-heading">
    <div class="section-heading">
      <p class="eyebrow">The current development model</p>
      <h2 id="open-development-heading">
        Agent-driven, human-directed, open to everyone
      </h2>
    </div>

    <div class="detail-grid">
      <article class="detail-card">
        <h3>Software workflows</h3>
        <p>
          Software development primarily uses Codex-driven agents under
          maintainer direction, with documented ownership, provenance review,
          tests, and repository validation. This describes the main workflow; it
          is not a claim that every line or commit was written by an agent.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="detail-card">
        <h3>Creator-directed world</h3>
        <p>
          People set the creative direction for maps, archetypes, stories,
          dialogue, graphics, animation, music, and sound effects. Generative
          tools may help a content creator shape terrain or develop writing.
          Generated visual and audio assets remain temporary, clearly marked
          design drop-ins and must be replaced for final use.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="detail-card">
        <h3>Human code is welcome</h3>
        <p>
          People can contribute code directly. Human-written and agent-written
          changes pass the same review, evidence-gated provenance, licensing,
          and validation gates; contributors do not need to use an agent.
        </p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section" aria-labelledby="follow-heading">
    <aside class="callout">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">Follow the journey</p>
        <h2 id="follow-heading">See what is happening now</h2>
        <p>
          Visit the project repositories for current plans, source, and ways to
          contribute.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="card-actions">
        <a
          class="button button--primary"
          href="https://github.com/atrinik/atrinik/issues/168"
          >Read the roadmap</a
        >
        <a class="button button--ghost" href="https://github.com/atrinik/"
          >Browse Atrinik on GitHub</a
        >
      </div>
    </aside>
  </section>
</Base>
